          freshness: 'current',
          script: "Now turn to Part one. You will hear a woman returning a faulty kettle to a shop. First, you have some time to look at questions one to ten.\n— Customer service, good morning.\n— Hello. I bought a kettle here and it has stopped heating.\n— I'm sorry to hear that. When did you buy it?\n— Three weeks ago, on the twelfth.\n— Do you have the receipt?\n— I have the email one on my phone.\n— That's fine, an email receipt is accepted. Can I take your name?\n— It's Adeyemi. A, D, E, Y, E, M, I.\n— Adeyemi. Thank you. Which kettle was it?\n— The Brantwood, the white one. It was thirty-four pounds.\n— And what exactly is wrong?\n— It switches on, the light comes on, but the water never gets hot.\n— Right. Within thirty days you may choose a refund or an exchange. After thirty days it goes for repair first.\n— I'd rather have a different model. The same one might do the same thing.\n— Then an exchange. The Brantwood is thirty-four and the next model up, the Halden, is forty-one, so there would be seven pounds to pay.\n— That's fine.\n— Have you brought the box?\n— No, I threw it away. Is that a problem?\n— Not for an exchange. It only matters for a refund, where we need the packaging.\n— Good. How long will it take?\n— Five minutes at the desk, but the Halden is not on the shelf today. It comes in on Thursday.\n— Can you hold one for me?\n— I can, for forty-eight hours. Come to the collection point at the back of the store, not this desk.\n— Will the guarantee start again on the new one?\n— It will. A replacement carries a fresh two-year guarantee from the day you collect it, and we register that for you at the counter.\n— That's good to know.\n— Do I bring the old kettle?\n— Please. We can't complete the exchange without it, and bring the charger cable as well if it is separate.\n— It isn't, it's attached. Thank you very much.\n— You're welcome. We'll see you on Thursday.",
