          script: "Now turn to Part one. You will hear a man joining a public library. First, you have some time to look at questions one to ten.\n— Good afternoon. I'd like to join the library.\n— Certainly. Have you lived in the city long?\n— About six weeks. I moved here for work.\n— That's fine. I'll need your name and an address.\n— My name is Ferreira. F, E, double R, E, I, R, A.\n— Ferreira. And the address?\n— Flat nine, Ashcroft House, Weston Road.\n— Flat nine, Ashcroft House. Do you have anything with that address on it? A bank letter or a bill?\n— I have a phone bill on my phone. Is that all right?\n— A photograph of one isn't accepted, I'm afraid. It has to be a paper copy, or a letter. A driving licence would do as well.\n— I can bring a bank letter tomorrow.\n— That's fine. I'll start the card now and you can activate it when you come back. Now, some details about borrowing. You may take out twelve items at a time.\n— Twelve. And for how long?\n— Books are three weeks, and you can renew twice. DVDs are one week and cannot be renewed at all.\n— Are there any charges?\n— Borrowing is free. There's a fine of ten pence a day on a late book, up to a maximum of five pounds.\n— And if I lose something?\n— Then you pay what it costs to replace it, plus a three-pound administration charge.\n— I understand. Do you have computers?\n— We do, on the first floor. Members get one hour a day free, and you book it at the desk upstairs, not here.\n— Is there anything on at the weekend? I have children.\n— There's a story session for under-fives every Saturday at eleven. It's popular, so come a few minutes early. There's also a homework club on Sundays, but that's for secondary pupils.\n— And can I return books to any branch?\n— To any of the five branches, yes, and there is a returns box outside this one for when we are closed.\n— Thank you. That's very helpful.",
