          freshness: 'current',
          script: "Now turn to Part one. You will hear a woman arranging a repair to a washing machine. First, you have some time to look at questions one to ten.\n— Appliance repairs, good morning.\n— Hello. My washing machine has stopped working and I'd like someone to look at it.\n— I can arrange that. Can I take your name?\n— It's Osei. O, S, E, I.\n— Thank you. And the make of the machine?\n— It's a Voller. I bought it about four years ago.\n— Do you know the model?\n— There's a number on the door. It says W, D, six-one-five-oh.\n— WD six-one-five-oh. What's the machine doing?\n— It fills with water and then stops. The drum doesn't turn at all.\n— And is there a light or a code showing?\n— Yes, there's an E and then the number four.\n— E-four usually means the motor, though it can be the belt. Either way the engineer will need to open it, so I'll book a full visit rather than a check.\n— How much is that?\n— The visit is fifty-five pounds, which includes the first half hour. Parts are extra. If it is the belt, the part is about twenty pounds; a motor is nearer a hundred and forty.\n— And when could someone come?\n— I have Wednesday morning or Friday afternoon this week.\n— Wednesday would be better.\n— Then I'll put you down for Wednesday, between eight and twelve. The engineer will ring about half an hour before.\n— Will I need to do anything before he arrives?\n— Please pull the machine out from the wall if you can, and empty the drum. If there's water in it, put a towel underneath.\n— The drum is full of water now. How do I empty it?\n— There's a small filter behind a flap at the bottom. Open it slowly with a bowl underneath. If you'd rather not, the engineer will do it, but it adds about twenty minutes.\n— I'll try. And how do I pay?\n— By card, to the engineer, on the day. We don't take cash and we don't invoice.",
