          level: 'B1',
          family: 'briefing',
          freshness: 'current',
          script: "Now turn to Part two. You will hear a woman welcoming new volunteers to a community garden. First, you have some time to look at questions eleven to twenty.\nGood morning, everyone, and welcome to Fenwick Community Garden. I'm Priya, and I coordinate the volunteers here. I'll take about ten minutes to tell you how the garden runs, and then we'll walk round it together.\nFirst, the practical things. We are open every day from eight in the morning until dusk, but volunteer sessions are on Tuesdays and Saturdays only. Tuesday sessions run from ten until one. Saturday sessions are longer, from nine until three, and lunch is provided on Saturdays but not on Tuesdays, so bring something with you if you come midweek.\nYou will have seen the sign-in book by the gate. Please use it every time. It is not for checking up on anyone; it is what our insurance requires, and if the book says nobody is here, then officially nobody is here.\nNow, the tools. The shed is unlocked during sessions and the code is only given to session leaders, so please don't take tools home even overnight. Everything comes back to the shed clean. There is a brush and a bucket by the door for that. The one thing I ask you to be careful with is the rotavator, which is heavy and is used by trained volunteers only.\nAbout the plots. The garden is divided into three areas. The beds nearest the gate are the community beds, and everything grown there is shared. The middle section is the orchard, which needs almost nothing from us except in autumn. The far end is the wildlife area, and that one is deliberately left rough. Please don't tidy it. Every year somebody weeds it with the best intentions, and every year we lose the frogs.\nWe do ask for one thing that surprises people. If you take vegetables home, write them in the produce book. We are not counting what anyone takes, and there is no limit. We report the total weight to the council, and that figure is what keeps our lease at a peppercorn rent.\nFinally, the children's sessions. Those run in the school holidays, on Wednesday mornings, and we need two extra adults each time. If you'd like to help with those, speak to Marcus rather than to me. He organises them and I never know the dates until he tells me.",
