          voice: { voiceId: "n5kSEVO24b8CF1IsU4B6", voiceIds: ["n5kSEVO24b8CF1IsU4B6", "GrVxA7Ub86nJH91Viyiv"], vendorName: "Dave - Deep, Warm and Approachable + James - English Storyteller", requestedVariety: "canadian", modelId: "eleven_flash_v2_5", renderedAt: "2026-08-31" },
          part: {"en":"Part 2","fr":"Partie 2"},
          speakers: 1,
          level: 'B1',
          family: 'briefing',
          freshness: 'current',
          script: "Now turn to Part two. You will hear a man explaining the arrangements at a residents' car park. First, you have some time to look at questions eleven to twenty.\nGood evening. Thank you for coming. I'm the building manager, and I've called this meeting because the car park arrangements change on the first of next month, and I would rather explain them now than answer forty emails afterwards.\nHere is what is changing and, just as importantly, what is not. The number of spaces is not changing. There are fifty-two, there have always been fifty-two, and nobody is losing a space. What changes is how they are allocated.\nUntil now, spaces have been numbered and each flat has had its own. That sounds fair and it has not worked, because about a third of the spaces stand empty on any given day while visitors circle the block. From the first, the parking becomes unnumbered. You park in any space that is free, and your permit is what entitles you to be there rather than a particular rectangle of tarmac.\nPermits arrive by post next week. Each flat gets one as before. If you want a second, there is a waiting list, and I will be honest with you: it is nineteen names long and it moves about twice a year.\nThe eight spaces along the north wall are the exception. Four of those are accessible bays and stay reserved, and they are enforced. The other four become visitor spaces, bookable through the building app for up to four hours. You book them under your own flat number, so if a visitor overstays it is your permit that gets the notice.\nCharging points. There are two, and they are staying where they are, but the rule around them changes. At present people leave a car on a charger overnight. From the first, a charging bay is for three hours, and after that it is an ordinary space and you may be asked to move.\nOne more thing, and it is the one that will cause the most argument. The old numbered spaces near the lift were the ones everyone wanted, and under the new system nobody owns them. They will go to whoever arrives first. I know that is not popular with the people who have had them for years, but the alternative is keeping a system where a third of the tarmac sits empty and our visitors park on the street.",
