          level: 'B1',
          family: 'briefing',
          freshness: 'current',
          script: "Now turn to Part two. You will hear a woman describing a summer activity programme for teenagers. First, you have some time to look at questions eleven to twenty.\nHello, and thanks for coming along this evening. I run the summer programme at the sports centre, and this is the meeting where parents ask me the questions the leaflet doesn't answer.\nThe programme runs for six weeks. It starts on the eighth of July. It is for young people between twelve and sixteen. We take a maximum of sixty a week. The first two weeks fill up. The last one stays quiet. So if you have any choice, book late rather than early.\nThe day starts at nine and finishes at four. We supervise from half past eight and until half past four. There is no extra charge for that. It is there because most of you work. Beyond those hours we cannot supervise. I would rather say that plainly now.\nThe activities split into three strands. Water sports are on Mondays and Wednesdays. They take place at the reservoir, not here. There is a coach at half past eight. It does not wait. Court sports are Tuesdays and Thursdays and are here in the main hall. Fridays are the trip day, and the destination changes each week.\nAbout cost. The week costs ninety-five pounds. That includes everything except the Friday trip. A trip costs between ten and twenty-five pounds. It depends where we go. You pay for it on the Monday of the same week. If a trip is cancelled for weather, you are refunded in full.\nLunch is not provided. There is a canteen and it is reasonable, but many young people bring their own, and there is a fridge they may use. We ask that nothing containing nuts comes into the building at all.\nNow the question everybody asks. Yes, they can leave at lunchtime if you have signed the form. No, we cannot let them go without it. A phone call from you on the day is not enough. The form has to be signed once, at the start, and it lasts the whole summer.\nBookings open on the fourteenth of May at nine in the morning, online only. We do not take bookings by phone, and I am afraid we cannot hold a place for anyone, including for the families who came last year.",
