          part: {"en":"Part 3","fr":"Partie 3"},
          speakers: 2,
          level: 'B2',
          family: 'discussion',
          freshness: 'current',
          script: "Now turn to Part three. You will hear a student, Owen, discussing a project with his tutor. First, you have some time to look at questions twenty-one to thirty.\n— Come in, both of you. How is the water usage project going?\n— Better than last week. We've got the survey back.\n— How many responses in the end?\n— Eighty-seven. We were hoping for two hundred, but the response rate from the halls was very low.\n— Eighty-seven is workable. What did the halls give you?\n— Only eleven. The rest came from the family housing. That's the thing we should say in the write-up, isn't it? That the sample isn't the campus, it's the family housing.\n— It is, and say it early rather than in a footnote. Now, what did you find?\n— The headline is that people who said they were most concerned about water used the most. Which is the opposite of what we expected.\n— Careful. Used the most in total, or the most per person?\n— In total.\n— And the family households are bigger.\n— Ah. So the concerned people might just be the people with more children.\n— That's what I'd want you to check before you write that sentence. Divide by household size and see whether it survives.\n— If it doesn't survive, do we still report it?\n— You report both. The raw figure and the per-person one, and you say which you think is the right unit and why. What you must not do is report only the one that makes the better story.\n— Fine. Then the second finding is about the shower timers. We handed out fifty and got thirty-one back with the diaries filled in.\n— That's a good return.\n— The average shower fell from nine minutes to six in the first week. But by week four it was back to eight.\n— That pattern is more interesting than the first-week figure, and it is the one people leave out. Why do you think it drifts back?\n— We asked. The most common answer was that they stopped looking at it.\n— Then say that, and say how you know. Now, timing. Your draft is due on the nineteenth?\n— The twenty-second.\n— Even better. Get the per-person recalculation done first, because if the first finding does not survive it, your whole introduction changes and there is no point polishing it before then.\n— One last thing. Should we put the diaries in an appendix?\n— Put a sample in, not all thirty-one. Three or four that show the range, and say how you chose them.\n— Chose them how?\n— However you like, as long as you say it. A reader can forgive a small sample and cannot forgive not knowing how it was picked.",
