          script: "Now turn to Part three. You will hear a student, Ines, talking to her tutor about a reading list. First, you have some time to look at questions twenty-one to thirty.\n— So, the reading list. You said it was too long.\n— It's forty items for eight weeks. Nobody reads forty items.\n— Nobody is meant to. Did you notice it is in three sections?\n— I saw the headings, but I assumed they were topics.\n— They are not. The first eight are essential, the next twelve are recommended, and the rest is there if you write on that topic.\n— That is not obvious from the document.\n— No, and that is fair criticism. I will put a sentence at the top before next year. What made you read it as one list?\n— The numbering. It runs from one to forty straight through, so it looks like a single sequence.\n— That is a good point and an easy fix.\n— Can I ask about the two book chapters in the essential eight? The library has one copy of each.\n— Both are scanned. They are in the module folder, not the library catalogue, which is probably why you didn't find them.\n— I looked in the catalogue for an hour.\n— Then that is the second thing I need to fix. Now, about the essay. You are choosing between the migration topic and the housing one?\n— Housing, I think. There's more written on it.\n— There is more written on it, which cuts both ways. It is easier to find sources and harder to say anything that has not been said.\n— Would you steer us away from it?\n— No. I would steer you towards a narrower version of it. Housing in general has been done. Housing policy in one city over one decade has not, and it is the same amount of reading.\n— That sounds more manageable, actually.\n— It usually is. One more thing: the word limit is two thousand, and the penalty above it is steep — five per cent for every hundred words. People lose a grade to that every year, almost always because they left the editing until the last evening.\n— Can we use sources that aren't on the list at all?\n— Please do. The list is a floor, not a ceiling, and the best essays every year use something I have not read.\n— How many extra would you expect?\n— I would not put a number on it. Two well-chosen ones are worth ten that are there to lengthen the bibliography, and a marker can tell the difference within a paragraph.\n— That's useful. Thank you.",
