          voice: { voiceId: "lgkJiqKJlFaaki1bxYFT", voiceIds: ["lgkJiqKJlFaaki1bxYFT", "n5kSEVO24b8CF1IsU4B6", "GrVxA7Ub86nJH91Viyiv"], vendorName: "Rebecca - Calm, Warm and Engaging + Dave - Deep, Warm and Approachable + James - English Storyteller", requestedVariety: "canadian", modelId: "eleven_flash_v2_5", renderedAt: "2026-08-31" },
          part: {"en":"Part 3","fr":"Partie 3"},
          speakers: 2,
          level: 'B2',
          family: 'discussion',
          freshness: 'current',
          script: "Now turn to Part three. You will hear a student, Danny, discussing a work placement with his course tutor. First, you have some time to look at questions twenty-one to thirty.\n— Sit down. You wanted to talk about the placement.\n— Yes. I've had two offers and I can't decide.\n— Tell me about them.\n— The first is a small architecture practice, six people, in the city centre. The second is a large engineering consultancy in Bristol, employing about four hundred professional staff.\n— And the work?\n— At the small practice I'd be doing a bit of everything. Drawings, site visits, some client meetings. At the large firm I'd be in one team, working on drainage.\n— Which one are you leaning towards?\n— The small one, because it sounds more interesting. But everyone tells me the big name looks better afterwards.\n— Let me push back on that. Whose name is on your degree certificate?\n— The university's.\n— And who reads your placement report?\n— My supervisor here.\n— So the name matters less than you have been told. What matters considerably more is what you can describe in an interview, and somebody who has attempted five different activities explains them more convincingly than somebody who has perfected one.\n— That's an odd way to put it.\n— It is, and it is not quite what I mean. I mean that breadth gives you more to say. But there is a serious argument on the other side, and you should hear it properly. Drainage is unglamorous, and it is simultaneously where the money is, so a summer of specialisation makes you employable in a way that a summer of variety does not.\n— So you are saying both.\n— I am saying the question is not which is better. It is which one you would still be glad of in two years. Now, some practical things. Are they both paid?\n— The large firm pays. The small one offers expenses only.\n— Then that is a substantial difference, and you should not pretend otherwise. How far away is Bristol for you?\n— An hour and a half each way.\n— Three hours a day. Add that up over ten weeks before you decide.\n— I hadn't.\n— Most people haven't. One last thing: the placement report is due two weeks after you finish, not at the start of term. People miss that every year.\n— Should I tell the one I turn down straight away?\n— Yes, and this week. They will have a second choice waiting and it is a small profession. A late refusal is remembered longer than a quick one.\n— And do I say why?\n— A sentence is plenty. You do not owe anybody a paragraph, and a long explanation usually reads as an apology for a decision that needed none.",
